<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helen Keller</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Helen Adams Keller</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helen Keller was an American deafblind author, disability rights advocate and political activist, born in Tuscumbia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. An illness at 19 months old left her blind and deaf, and from the age of seven she was taught language, including reading and writing, by Anne Sullivan, her lifelong companion. She attended Radcliffe College and became the first deafblind person in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to earn a college diploma. She wrote 14 books, among them The Story of My Life (1903), and campaigned for people with disabilities and for women's suffrage, labour rights and world peace. She joined the Socialist Party of America in 1909 and was a founding member of the American Civil Liberties Union.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helen-keller.jpg" alt="Helen Keller" width="330" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helen_Keller_(circa_1904).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helen Keller was born on 27 June 1880 at Tuscumbia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, to Arthur Henley Keller, editor of the Tuscumbia North Alabamian and a former captain in the Confederate Army, and Catherine Everett Adams Keller, the daughter of a Confederate general. At 19 months she contracted an illness that doctors described as &quot;an acute congestion of the stomach and the brain&quot;; contemporary doctors believe it may have been meningitis, rubella or scarlet fever.</p><p>She entered the Perkins Institute for the Blind in May 1888 and later attended the Wright-Humason School for the Deaf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and the Cambridge School for Young Ladies. Mark Twain introduced her to the Standard Oil magnate Henry Huttleston Rogers, who with his wife paid for her education at Radcliffe College, where she graduated in 1904 at the age of 24 as a member of Phi Beta Kappa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Story of My Life (1903) was written during her college years with help from Anne Sullivan and her husband John Macy. In a 1907 article she drew attention to the fact that many cases of childhood blindness could be prevented by washing every newborn baby's eyes with a disinfectant solution, a measure that was then widely adopted. She also wrote The World I Live In (1908) and Out of the Dark (1913), a series of essays on socialism.</p><p>She travelled to twenty-five countries giving lectures, and was a suffragist, pacifist and supporter of birth control. In 1915 she and George A. Kessler founded Helen Keller International, devoted to research in vision, health and nutrition, and in 1916 she sent money to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>. After Anne Sullivan died in 1936, Polly Thomson, hired in about 1914 to keep house, remained her companion until her own death in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Keller had a series of strokes in 1961 and spent her last years at home. On 14 September 1964 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson">Lyndon B. Johnson</a> awarded her the Presidential Medal of Freedom. She died in her sleep on 1 June 1968 at her home, Arcan Ridge, in Easton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, aged 87, and her ashes were buried at the Washington National Cathedral beside Sullivan and Thomson.</p><p>William Gibson's play The Miracle Worker reached Broadway in 1959 and was filmed in 1962. Her birthplace is a National Historic Landmark and a house museum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> put her on its state quarter in 2003, the only circulating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> coin to carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braille">braille</a>, and in 2009 a bronze statue of her was placed in the National Statuary Hall Collection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helen Keller” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
